  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 239 PM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nGEFS and EC 500-mb height mean-spread guidance is supportive of a \nlongwave upper-level ridge centered across the High Plains for the \nentirety of the long term period. The feature appears strong enough \nthat shortwave troughs from the Aleutian Islands will be redirected \nnortheastward before they encroach into the United States. However, \nbroad surface lows in association with these troughs look to favor \nsoutherly surface flow across the forecast region at least through \nSunday night. Hot conditions appear favored over the weekend, with \nhighs forecast in the mid 90s to lower 100s Saturday, and upper 90s \nto mid 100s Sunday. Heat index values in the mid 100s are possible, \nespecially Sunday along and east of Kansas Highway 25 where dew \npoints in the upper 50s to lower 60s are possible. NBM guidance \nsuggests over a 90% chance for much of Northwest Kansas and \nSouthwest Nebraska to experience temperatures over 100 degrees \nSunday afternoon. Confidence in a Heat Advisory being needed Sunday \nis around 30%. In addition to the hot weather, dry conditions are \nforecast as well. Relative humidities (RH) are forecast to be low, \nespecially on Sunday when they drop into the upper teens across \nportions of Eastern Colorado. Even so, critical fire weather \nconditions are not yet a concern, as wind gusts are forecast to max \nout in the low to mid 20 mph. NBM guidance suggests a 20% chance or \nless across Eastern Colorado that wind gusts will meet critical fire \nweather criteria (25 mph or greater). As such, confidence in a Red \nFlag Warning being needed across this zone is less than 5%. \n\n\nGEFS 850-mb height mean-spread guidance shows a stationary low \ndeveloping across portions of Utah and Colorado Monday. This may \nallow for showers and thunderstorms to return to the area Monday \nthrough Wednesday. NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance shows the \nhighest chances to receive greater than 0.1 inches of precipitation \nacross Eastern Colorado, with some areas over a 40% chance each day. \nThunderstorms are not favored to become severe during this period, \nas GFS and EC model soundings show 0-6km shear maxing out around 30 \nkts. However, it may be important to note that GFS soundings are \nshowing a deep inverted-V profile up to nearly 500-mb. If \nthunderstorms could occur with this sounding profile, microbursts \nwould be possible, with a chance of experiencing severe winds. \nConfidence in severe weather occurring is about 5% for each day \nMonday through Wednesday. Temperatures in the mid 100s may continue \ninto Monday as well. NBM guidance suggests between a 70-90% chance \nfor temperatures to rise above 100 degrees. Confidence in a Heat \nAdvisory being needed Monday is around 15%. High temperatures begin \nslightly dropping on Tuesday into the mid 90s to lower 100s, and low \nto mid 90s Wednesday, which would dramatically decrease the \nlikelihood of further Heat Advisories.",
